speechtext:
I was thinking of
pressed hay. I do not think the inspection will have much to do with loose hay, because, so far as loose hay is concerned, the buyer and the seller usually come together and the buyer sees what he is getting. I do not see how you are going to inspect properly the baled hay. Clause (b) reads:
No. 1 Timothy shall he timothy with not more than one-eighth of clover or other tame grasses mixed, of good colour, sound and well cured ;
Who is going to be the judge as to the percentages? Then clause (c) reads:
No. 2 Timothy shall be timothy with not more than one-third of clover or other tame grasses mixed, of good colour, sound and well cured ;
It seems to me this puts the whole question of pressing and marketing hay in a state of chaos, unless you are going to have inspectors who will be willing and ready to go out at the beck and call of every farmer throughout the Dominion. Who is going to pay the expense of this inspection? It is all very well to be setting grades on hay, but the number of grades of hay may be legion. In this section we have eleven grades mentioned. Who is to determine these grades? The farmer is running ,the risk of having a penalty imposed upon him if he should press two or three tons of hay before the inspector arrives.
